WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.070
Two service routes each show an eighty-percent completion rate.

2
00:00:03.090 --> 00:00:06.590
Have they offered equally accessible services? We cannot answer

3
00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:09.010
that from the percentage alone. We need to know

4
00:00:09.030 --> 00:00:12.090
who was included, what completion means, and what happened

5
00:00:12.110 --> 00:00:15.610
before people reached the point being counted. Understanding Evidence

6
00:00:15.630 --> 00:00:18.290
and Asking Better Equity Questions begins with that kind

7
00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:19.270
of careful attention.

8
00:00:20.060 --> 00:00:23.200
Evidence helps staff move beyond an impression, but a

9
00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:25.600
number or a personal account always answers a particular

10
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:28.940
question. Using it well means understanding its value and

11
00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.900
its limits, then deciding what further information would improve

12
00:00:31.920 --> 00:00:34.840
the work. The course's comparison uses eight recorded completions

13
00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:37.880
among ten enrolled people in each route. The arithmetic

14
00:00:37.900 --> 00:00:41.340
is straightforward: eight out of ten is eighty percent.

15
00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:43.680
The harder question is about the people who never

16
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:44.100
enrolled.

17
00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.370
Were they eligible but unable to reach an entry

18
00:00:47.390 --> 00:00:50.170
point? Did they choose another option? We have not

19
00:00:50.190 --> 00:00:53.230
been given that information. Equal completion among enrolled people

20
00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:56.270
does not establish equal access before enrollment. You will

21
00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:59.790
practice separating an observation, a possible explanation, and a

22
00:00:59.810 --> 00:01:03.330
decision. The recorded rates are an observation. A claim

23
00:01:03.350 --> 00:01:05.710
about why people did not enroll is an explanation

24
00:01:05.730 --> 00:01:06.670
that needs evidence.

25
00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:09.840
A decision about what to change must also consider

26
00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:14.580
purpose, alternatives, and responsibility. Keeping those distinctions clear makes

27
00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:18.000
an analysis more useful, not less decisive. It helps

28
00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.820
colleagues see what the recommendation rests on. There is

29
00:01:20.840 --> 00:01:23.740
a workplace example too. A development report records who

30
00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.300
attended a learning opportunity but not who received usable

31
00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:29.140
notice or could apply. Attendance can tell a team

32
00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.360
something about participation.

33
00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.310
It cannot, by itself, tell the whole story of

34
00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:36.830
opportunity. A supervisor or analyst may need to examine

35
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:40.470
the earlier invitation and application experience before explaining who

36
00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:43.410
was absent. Personal accounts add a different kind of

37
00:01:43.430 --> 00:01:46.590
knowledge. In another case, a participant says an online

38
00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.730
confirmation page did not work with their technology. The

39
00:01:49.750 --> 00:01:52.930
report records no response. The account offers a specific

40
00:01:52.950 --> 00:01:55.250
question about the route and what the record means.

41
00:01:55.767 --> 00:01:58.167
It should not be discarded because it comes from

42
00:01:58.187 --> 00:02:01.627
one person, or expanded into the explanation for every

43
00:02:01.647 --> 00:02:05.087
missing response. Its contribution is valuable at the scope

44
00:02:05.107 --> 00:02:08.287
it actually describes. The course will help you ask

45
00:02:08.307 --> 00:02:10.947
what a number is a share of, whether definitions

46
00:02:10.967 --> 00:02:13.947
and periods match, and whose experience may be missing.

47
00:02:14.566 --> 00:02:17.206
You will consider why an overall result can hide

48
00:02:17.226 --> 00:02:20.906
different experiences and why a before-and-after change does not

49
00:02:20.926 --> 00:02:23.946
prove its own cause. Culture briefs and community knowledge

50
00:02:23.966 --> 00:02:26.846
can widen the questions, while direct evidence is still

51
00:02:26.866 --> 00:02:29.746
needed to examine them. Your final practice is a

52
00:02:29.766 --> 00:02:32.966
short evidence note in plain language. It should explain

53
00:02:32.986 --> 00:02:35.866
what is known, what remains uncertain, and which next

54
00:02:35.886 --> 00:02:37.406
question matters to the decision.

55
00:02:38.086 --> 00:02:41.626
That skill is useful when reviewing service access, staff

56
00:02:41.646 --> 00:02:45.506
opportunities, a policy proposal, or a report for leadership.

57
00:02:46.026 --> 00:02:47.806
You do not have to make the evidence say

58
00:02:47.826 --> 00:02:50.346
more than it does. A careful account can give

59
00:02:50.366 --> 00:02:52.826
people a stronger basis for deciding what to examine

60
00:02:52.846 --> 00:02:55.526
and what to do next. Try the evidence note

61
00:02:55.546 --> 00:02:59.046
this way: “Both routes recorded eight completions among ten

62
00:02:59.066 --> 00:02:59.886
enrolled people.

63
00:03:00.465 --> 00:03:02.725
We do not know who tried to enroll or

64
00:03:02.745 --> 00:03:05.585
whether the entry route worked for them. Before recommending

65
00:03:05.605 --> 00:03:09.365
a change, examine the invitation and enrollment experience alongside

66
00:03:09.385 --> 00:03:12.565
the completion record.” The note keeps the useful finding

67
00:03:12.585 --> 00:03:15.785
and gives a specific next inquiry. It neither declares

68
00:03:15.805 --> 00:03:19.025
equal access nor assumes a barrier occurred. That is

69
00:03:19.045 --> 00:03:20.365
the distinction you will practice.
